<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning from Mistakes</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Next Time, I Will Study Harder to Avoid...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify the main ideas of spoken news about familiar topics. Interactive: Contribute to simple discussions on a variety of issues.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify the main ideas in spoken news reports or classroom stories about familiar learning experiences by answering comprehension questions (e.g., "What did Maria learn from her mistake?" "She should ask for help."). Can follow and contribute to discussions on how to improve study habits by sharing past experiences, making suggestions, and describing future actions (e.g., "What did you learn from your mistake?" "I forgot to review my notes." "What will you do next time?" "I will start studying earlier."). Can respond to questions in a discussion about learning experiences by recognizing suggestions (e.g., "What advice was given?" "I should practice every day.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing new vocabulary and pronunciation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows a picture of a student looking sad or frustrated while holding a report card with a low grade. The teacher asks, "What do you think happened to this student?" and "What mistake might they have made?" Students listen to the questions and offer short, simple answers (e.g., "Bad grades," "Forgot homework," "Didn't study"). This prompts initial reflection on the theme 'Learning from Mistakes' and 'Next Time, I Will Study Harder to Avoid...'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes the new vocabulary words on the board: <i>mistakes, grades, study, habits, homework, feedback, progress, improvement, success, better, successful, challenging, carefully, well</i>. The teacher reads each word clearly, modeling pronunciation. Special attention is given to the 'ed' endings: /d/ in "improved," /ɪd/ in "wanted," and /t/ in "practiced" (when introduced later in context). The teacher also models minimal pairs for phonemic awareness: e.g., saying "they" and "day," asking students to listen for the difference. The teacher provides simple definitions or uses gestures for each new word.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I forget to do my homework, is that a mistake or success?" (Expected answer: A mistake.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When my grades go up, is that progress or bad habits?" (Expected answer: Progress.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1: 0-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning from Mistakes." Comprehension tasks based on authentic audio.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio story (1-1.5 minutes) about a middle school student named Alex who made a mistake on a recent challenging science project, received low grades, and learned a valuable lesson. The audio uses past simple to describe the events, and future tense ("I will...") and "should" for reflection and advice. Genre: Personal narrative/classroom story. Register: Informal. Format: Audio recording.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret and analyze information from various oral texts to solve problems and make decisions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> The teacher asks, "What was Alex's main problem?" and "What did Alex learn?" Students listen to the audio for the general idea and answer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (Specific details):</b> Students receive a simple worksheet with True/False questions or fill-in-the-blank sentences based on the audio. E.g., "Alex got good grades on the science project. (True/False)," "Alex learned that he _____ study earlier." (should/will). This focuses on identifying specific information and target vocabulary (e.g., *mistakes, grades, challenging, learned, study, feedback*).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1: 10-20 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening. Controlled and semi-controlled activities to practice target grammar, vocabulary, and pronunciation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening comprehension, past simple for experiences, future tense ("will") for improvements, "should" for advice. Vocabulary: <i>mistakes, grades, study, habits, homework, feedback, progress, improvement, success, better, successful, challenging, carefully, well</i>. Pronunciation of past tense verbs ending "ed": /d/, /ɪd/, /t/. Pragmatic functions: Reflecting on learning, giving advice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listening for Vocabulary Match:</b> The teacher plays parts of the audio story again. Students listen for specific target vocabulary words (e.g., *mistakes, homework, challenging, grades*). After listening to each segment, students match the heard word to its definition or a synonym on their worksheet. This reinforces listening for specific information and vocabulary recognition.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Past Tense Sounds" Listening Drill:</b> The teacher reads aloud a list of past tense verbs, some from the audio (e.g., *learned, decided, practiced, improved, wanted, failed*). Students listen carefully and sort the verbs into three columns on their worksheet based on the '-ed' pronunciation: /t/, /d/, or /ɪd/. (e.g., "practiced" /t/, "improved" /d/, "wanted" /ɪd/). The teacher models the sounds again as needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Advice Gap-Fill Listening:</b> Students listen to short incomplete sentences or dialogues where a friend is describing a problem related to study habits. They must listen and complete the advice given using "should" or "will" based on what they hear. For example, "My grades are low." (Audio: "You ____ study more.") or "I forgot my homework." (Audio: "Next time, I ____ write it in my planner.") This practices listening for suggestions and future actions.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1: 20-35 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario. Focus on fluency and collaborative interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Name: "My Learning Experience" Peer Discussion</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student takes turns sharing a past academic challenge or mistake they made (e.g., getting a low grade on a test, not understanding a topic, forgetting homework). The other student listens carefully, asks clarifying questions (e.g., "What did you learn from this mistake?"), and then provides constructive feedback and advice using "You should..." and describes future improvements using "Next time, I will..." or "You will...". The focus is on active listening, understanding the peer's experience, and formulating appropriate suggestions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Structured peer interviews/discussion. Students use the target vocabulary (<i>mistakes, grades, study, habits, homework, feedback, progress, improvement, success</i>) and grammar (past simple, future 'will', 'should') in their interactions. The teacher circulates, listening to student conversations and providing support as needed, particularly focusing on effective listening and appropriate responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Interaction:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Last semester, my grades in Math were really low. I didn't understand the homework."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B (listening): "Oh, that sounds challenging. What did you learn from this mistake?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "I learned I should ask for help quickly."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Exactly! You should ask your teacher for feedback. Next time, I will try to study with a friend, that might help your improvement."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1: 35-45 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of listening and interactive communication. Student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the peer discussion, the teacher uses an observation checklist to assess students' listening comprehension and their ability to contribute to discussions effectively. Specifically, the teacher notes: (1) if students accurately grasp their partner's mistake and learning, and (2) if they provide appropriate advice and future actions using target language. A few pairs might be asked to share a brief summary of their discussion with the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Active Listening:</b> Student listens attentively and demonstrates understanding (e.g., asks relevant follow-up questions, summarizes partner's point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Student uses at least 3-4 new vocabulary words (e.g., *mistakes, grades, habits, improvement*) in their responses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Appropriate Grammar:</b> Student correctly uses past simple for experiences, 'should' for advice, and 'will' for future actions/improvements.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Overall clear pronunciation, with effort to pronounce '-ed' endings and minimal pairs correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and Feedback:</b> Student engages respectfully and offers constructive feedback.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I learned today is _____."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I learn about listening to others' mistakes and giving advice?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1: 45-50 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson, and connection to the next lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to others was easy, and what was challenging today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can listening carefully help me learn from my own mistakes and make progress?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The listening activities worked well, and students demonstrated understanding of the main ideas and target vocabulary. The pronunciation drill for '-ed' endings was useful, but some minimal pairs were still challenging. Students actively participated in the peer discussion, showing good interactive listening.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Adjustments for next time: Provide more visual cues or simpler sentence structures for the minimal pair exercise. Offer sentence starters for giving advice during the production stage to scaffold language use further for some students.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), students will read a text based on a similar scenario, reinforcing the same core vocabulary and grammar structures about learning from mistakes and making improvements. They will see the words they heard today in a new context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1: 50 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>